Secured and Monitored Web Infrastructure

The setup

1-Load Balancer (HAProxy)

-Same as before but now has SSL certificate installed
-Handles HTTPS connections from users
-Protected by first firewall

2-Server A

-Runs Nginx web server
-Runs application server with code
-Has MySQL database
-Protected by second firewall
-Has monitoring client installed

3-Server B

-Same setup as Server A
-Protected by third firewall
-Also has monitoring client


Why add these new parts?

-Three firewalls -> each protects a different piece. First one guards the load balancer from internet attacks. Other two protect each web server from direct attacks
-SSL certificate -> encrypts all traffic so hackers cant read user data. Makes browsers show lock icon and Google ranks HTTPS sites higher
-Three monitoring clients -> like security cameras watching everything. They track performance, errors, and traffic to catch problems early


What firewalls do

-Act like bouncers checking every network request
-Block bad IP addresses and suspicious traffic
-Only allow web traffic on ports 80 and 443
-Stop hackers from scanning for weaknesses

Why HTTPS matters

-Regular HTTP sends everything in plain text that anyone can read
-HTTPS encrypts data so only you and the website understand it
-Proves youre talking to the real website not a fake one
-Required for passwords and personal info

What monitoring does

-Tells us how many visitors we have right now
-Shows if servers are running out of memory or disk space
-Tracks how fast pages load
-Catches error messages users might see
-Detects if someone is attacking the site

How monitoring collects data

-Small programs called agents run on each server
-They check CPU usage, memory, disk space constantly
-Read log files from web servers and databases
-Send all info securely to monitoring service like Sumologic
-We get charts and alerts when something goes wrong

Monitoring web server QPS

-Enable nginx status page in config
-Set monitoring tool to check it every minute
-Count requests and calculate per second
-Set alerts for high or low traffic
-Track at load balancer too for total numbers


Big Picture

-User enters www.foobar.com
-Browser makes HTTPS connection
-First firewall checks the traffic
-Load balancer gets request and picks a server
-Request goes through another firewall to chosen server
-Nginx handles it, app server processes it, database provides data
-Response goes back through same path encrypted


Issues with this setup

-SSL termination problem -> traffic is encrypted to load balancer but plain text after that. Someone inside network could spy on it
-Single database writer -> only one database accepts new data. If it fails the whole site becomes read-only
-Everything on same servers -> web server, app, and database all compete for resources. Cant scale parts separately and harder to troubleshoot problems
